Certainly, here is an example of how a table of contents (ToC) can be generated automatically in a LaTeX document using the necessary commands:

1. Begin your LaTeX document by adding the document class at the beginning. For example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
```

This tells LaTeX that you want to create an article-style document.

2. After declaring the document class, include the following markup to create a ToC:

```latex
\begin{document}
  \tableofcontents % optional, but commonly placed in the document preamble using '\clearpage' or '\newpage' commands before to start a new page

  % From here, you include the content of your document within the LaTeX environment, which will be added to the ToC automatically.

  \section{Section 1}
  Content of Section 1

  \section{Section 2}
  Content of Section 2

  \subsection{Subsection 2.1}
  Content of Subsection 2.1

  \subsection{Subsection 2.2}
  Content of Subsection 2.2

  \subsection{Subsection 2.3}
  Content of Subsection 2.3

  \subsection{Subsection 2.4}
  Content of Subsection 2.4

  \section{Section 3}
  Content of Section 3

  \subsection{Subsection 3.1}
  Content of Subsection 3.1

  \subsection{Subsection 3.2}
  Content of Subsection 3.2

  \subsection{Subsection 3.3}
  Content of Subsection 3.3

\end{document}
```

Note: I've included the ToC markup `\tableofcontents` at the start of the document (in the document preamble) for illustrative purposes only. In practice, you may wish to place it later in your actual LaTeX file as a way to start a new page before the ToC appears.

3. By default, LaTeX will automatically generate a ToC for you as long as you include relevant section and subsections using commands like these:

- To add a first-level section in your document (assuming the name of the `\section` command is `\secsymbol{section-title}`):

  ```latex
  \section{First-level section title}
  Content for the section
  ```

- To add a second-level section (assuming the name of the `\subsection` command is `\subsymbol{subsection-title}`):

  ```latex
  \subsection{Second-level subsection title}
  Content for the subsection
  ```

4. To customize how your ToC looks, you can add the following commands within the braces of `\tableofcontents`. Here are a few examples:

```latex
\tableofcontents

% Example 1: Use only dot leaders
\usepackage{tocloft} % include the package to allow this to work
\renewcommand{\cftdotsep}{\cftnodots}

% Example 2: Use a specific ToC entry format
\usepackage{tocloft}
\newlength{\mylen}
\settowidth{\mylen}{Chapter X} % Example a specific chapter name
\renewcommand{\cftchapfont}{\rmfamily\bfseries}
\renewcommand{\cftchapnumfont}{\sffamily\hfill}
\renewcommand{\cftchappagefont}{\sffamily}
\renewcommand{\cftchapafterpawn}{\enspace}
\renewcommand{\cftchapterpresnum}{Chapter \textit{}
\renewcommand{\cftchapteraftersnum}{ }
\settowidth{\mylen}{\cftchapterpresnum\cftchapnumfont\cftchapteraftersnum}
\repositionfloat[\movefloat][\mylen][tocloft]

% Example 3: Use a different ToC style using the `titletoc` package
\usepackage:titletoc % include the package to allow this to work
\titlecontents{section}
[0em]{}
{\contentslabel{1em}}
{\contentsmargin{}}
{\normalsize\itshape}
```

Note: These examples are separate and can be used independently. You may need to include the appropriate packages for these commands to work (e.g., `tocloft`, `titletoc`, and `tocloft` for Examples 1, 2, and 3, respectively).

In the above example, the ToC will be generated automatically once you have included LaTeX sections (like the ones shown above) with their corresponding titles, even if you don't explicitly call the `\tableofcontents` command.

The resulting automatically-generated ToC will depend on your specific document class and the options you choose to customize it. However, here is an example of what the ToC might look like based on the given LaTeX code:

```
Contents

1 First-level section title                   1
   Subsection 2.1                               2.1
   Subsection 2.2                               2.2
   Subsection 2.3                               2.3
   Subsection 2.4                               2.4

3.0 Chapter 1                                    1
3.0 Chapter 2                                    2

1.0 Chapter X                                       1
  1.1 Section 1                                    1.1

```

This example assumes that the document class provides numbered sections and chapters, with the first two numbering levels displayed as "1. " and "3.0", respectively (these numbers are set using the appropriate sectioning commands: `\section{}` for level 1 and `\chapter{}` for level 2). It also assumes that the ToC has not been customized to use a different method for formatting the ToC entries, such as dot leaders (`\cftdotsep`) or specific fonts for the ToC entries (as demonstrated in Example 2).

Note: This is just an example of what the ToC might look like based on the given LaTeX code. Depending on your document class, the formatting and appearance of the ToC may vary significantly from this example. Additionally, the ToC generated may include section and subsection numbers, but it will not include chapter numbers if your document class does not use them. If your document class uses a different numbering format, the ToC will reflect that format accordingly. Finally, note that the specific numbers in the example (e.g., "1", "2", "2.1", "2.2", etc.) will be replaced with the appropriate numbering based on the document class and any customization commands used.

